es needed  more flexibility to rebuild their  economies  and chose to peg their currencies to the  dollar  [Applause]  [Music]  that's because the u.s had the most gold  at the time  a strong dollar can be good or bad  depending on who you are  japan needs the dollar to be stronger  against the yen so american consumers  can afford more japanese products  but a country like turkey would rather a  weaker u.s currency  because banks and businesses there hold  a lot of debt in dollars  in other countries a strong dollar can  fuel inflation  in argentina that pushes up prices for  basic things like food  and in the u.s a strong greenback makes  imported goods cheaper  but it can harm u.s manufacturers by  making their goods more expensive abroad  overall though the dollar is largely  seen as a safe currency  during the pandemic it actually rose  it's the worst economic crisis in the  last 70 years  and there's one safe haven everyone is  banking on and it's not gold  it's the dollar lately the us dollar has  gone up and down  that has to do with all sorts of things  even a tweet from president trump can  help  or hurt the exchange rate but a lot  rides on how the u.s  economy performs so how is it doing  economies are measured using gdp which  is the total value of goods and services  produced in that country  over a period of time and you can see  that over the last 10 years the us  economy  has been growing last year gdp rose by  just over two percent  but it took a while to get there  [Music]  like the rest of the world the us  economy was devastated by the financial  crisis in 2008.  president george w bush started the  cleanup shoring up banks and rescuing  u.s companies  the federal reserve also played a  massive role  buying bonds and keeping interest rates  low  and all that continued under president  barack obama congress  spent hundreds of billions of dollars on  things like social security  and tax relief by 2009 the u.s had  started its longest economic expansion  on record then president donald trump  stepped in  from the instant i took office i moved  rapidly to revive the us economy  the economy did keep growing but the  four to six percent boost he promised  well that didn't happen he came into  office and inherited an economy had been  strongly growing  since 2010 so for six or seven years  growth was already baked in trump added  his own touch  he cut regulations to make doing  business easier  and got congress to pass tax cuts for  american workers and corporations  the promise there was to help businesses  reinvest money to create jobs  but it didn't always work that way a lot  of those companies use those tax savings  to buy back stocks and boost their value  instead  then there's trump's trade policy of  putting america first  he ripped up international trade deals  saying they were unfair  and hurt american manufacturing the  united states  has lost about 5 million manufacturing  jobs over the last 20 years  and about 91 000 factories we've seen  over the past few years um  tensions arise between us and between  some of our  trading partners and one of the things  that this current administration has  done  is put a focus on china i am the chosen  one  somebody had to do it so i'm taking on  china  and that led to a trade war the u.s  slapped penalties on hundreds of  billions of dollars worth of chinese  goods  china responded with their own china  also found ways around some of those  tariffs  they have succeeded in reducing imports  from china  but china has just rerouted those  imports through other countries the main  thing about trump's tenure was that the  american public started to feel a whole  lot better about the economy  and that had an important effect when  consumers feel confident about their  financial prospects  they're more likely to spend they're  more likely to go out and buy things on  credit  so the economy was in good shape and the  u.s jobs market was on fire  then covert 19 came along and pushed the  economy  off a cliff stocks crashing to their  worst losses in over 30 years  the u.s economy slid 5 in the first  quarter when it officially fell into  recession  then in the second quarter it nose dived  at a record annual rate of more than 30  we went from some of the lowest  unemployment rates in our nation's  history  to levels that have not been seen in  most of our lifetime certainly not  in in my lifetime the pandemic still  isn't over  but the focus now is on the economic  recovery in the third quarter of 2020  the economy bounced back a record 33  percent  the federal reserve helped by unleashing  a range of measures to keep credit  flowing to businesses and households  like cutting interest rates to make  borrowing cheaper congress also  allocated trillions of dollars in aid to  keep workers and businesses afloat  and jump start growth that's a lot of  money  that was a big help to u.s households  and a big boon to the economy because it  helped consumer spending  as you've seen some states reopen but  you've actually seen a  very sharp recovery the stock market  also came roaring back  one reason was that tech companies  benefited from the shift to people  working and  learning from home you know what else is  up house prices  mortgage rates are at a record low and  lockdowns have inspired many homeowners  to sell their digs for bigger ones in  the suburbs  so people who own shares or homes are  doing okay  but what about the rest while some  economists are saying  this is the most unequal recovery in  american history  those at the top those who can  telecommute who can  do their business by zoom are doing fine  in fact  many of those those individuals are  continuing to receive incomes and yet  they're spending less  so they're saving more and so their  wealth is going up  contrast that to somebody who cannot  work remotely  they're not doing well right now there's  a survey out that showed  that one in three renters did not pay  their rent on time  in the first week of september we also  see  very very sharp racial disparities as  well we also know that economic recovery  is starting to slow down  covet 19 is still spreading some of that  financial help we talked about has  expired  and republicans and democrats in  congress can't agree on the next aid  package  federal reserve chairman jerome powell  warned that without more  aid coming from the federal government  the u.s economic recovery is in jeopardy  the outlook for the economy is  extraordinarily uncertain there are  still roughly 11 million people still  out of work due to the pandemic  you don't want to see the recovery going  off the rails in the world's largest  economy  getting the us economy back to its  pre-pandemic strength  could take years and how long the virus  sticks around will make a difference  we know the world economy is  interconnected it's like an ecosystem  and on that basis the us needs to do  well  for all of us to turn the corner  [Music]  if you want to get a better  understanding of the stories in the news  you can find all of our episodes on  youtube just look for start here  you can also find us on al jazeera's  facebook page and  on our website aljazeera.com make sure  to subscribe wherever you're watching  and we'll see you next week  [Music]

